.TH WALK 5
.SH NAME
walk \- descend a directory hierarchy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Twalk tag[2] fid[4] newfid[4] nwname[2] nwname*(wname[s])
.B size[4] Rwalk tag[2] nwqid[2] nwqid*(wqid[13])
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Twalk
carries an existing
.I fid
and a proposed
.I newfid
that the client wishes to bind to the result of walking
.I nwname
path elements,
.IR wname ,
from the file
.I fid
names.
.I Newfid
must not already be in use unless it is the same as
.IR fid .
.I Fid
must be a directory, unless
.I nwname
is zero, and must not have been opened for I/O:
a walk rebinds a fid, and a fid held open for reading must not move
under its reader.
See
.IR open (5).
.PP
The name
.B ..
means the parent directory.
The name
.B .
is not sent in a
.BR Twalk :
a client that means the current file sends
.I nwname
zero, which binds
.I newfid
to the same file as
.I fid
without a message reaching a server for it.
.PP
A short
.B Rwalk
(fewer qids than
.I nwname
named)
is a success carrying a partial result, not an error.
.I Newfid
is bound only when
.I nwqid
equals
.IR nwname ,
that is, only when every element walked.
Only a walk that fails on the first element is an error reply.
Getting this backwards turns every "no such file" into a protocol
failure instead of an ordinary
.BR ENOENT .
.PP
Vectra bounds a walk at sixteen elements per message, the constant
.B MAX_WALK_ELEMENTS
in
.IR vectra9 (2).
A path with more components is walked as more than one
.BR Twalk .
.PP
A walk may cross from one server to another partway through, when an
element it reaches is a mount point.
That crossing happens in the namespace layer, not on the wire:
a single
.B Twalk
message never leaves the server it was sent to.
See
.IR vfs (9).
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/proto.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/walk.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (5),
.IR attach (5),
.IR open (5),
.IR clunk (5),
.IR vectra9 (2),
.IR vfs (9)
